22 straight years of brutal drought have  just pummeled lake mead one of the  largest reservoirs in the whole world  but to paraphrase mark twain reports of  its death have been greatly exaggerated  marina operators at the lake say bad  publicity has just crippled their bottom  line but as they tell george knapp  they're still open for business  the lake has always been it's either  feast or famine bob gripentog's family  has operated the lake mead marina since  1957 and has learned to roll with  nature's punches in some years the water  level rises for most of the last 22  years it has fallen the massive  reservoir is doing what it was created  to do act as a water bank during dry  years though the driest period in 12  centuries has strenuously tested the  lake's limits this year is definitely  special compared to what it has been  since the early 60s every drop of 40  feet in the water level creates a  thousand feet of beach in front of the  marina so far this year the families had  to move the entire operation further out  into the lake eight times each move cost  around a hundred grand mainly because of  the need to extend lines for power water  sewer and fuel the business has always  been able to absorb the financial hit  because boaters and visitors usually  continue to pour in spend money in the  restaurant and store and launch their  boats but this year is different just  the number of folks that come out to  lake mead internationally wise and and  visit us has gone down  probably 50 percent the reason isn't  hard to identify media coverage has been  relentless the comparative satellite  photos of the shrinking shoreline  stories about bodies found in barrels  the image of a formerly submerged world  war ii era boat now high and dry and the  implication that the lake is suddenly  dangerous because of obstacles lurking  just under the waves all of that  information has convinced potential  visitors that the lake is shut down  the months-long onslaught of negative  media articles both nationally and  internationally have created a false  impression that lake mead has dwindled  down to a deadpool mud puddle but when  you're out here on the water and look  around you can see this is still one  enormous lake those are the stories that  really create us hardships because  people do get the idea that there is no  water here even at these levels we have  over  around 500 miles of shoreline  the water  where we're at right now is over 100  feet deep if you get out into the basin  area it'll be  350 even 400 feet deep gripen talk says  you'd be far more likely to find a dead  body four-wheeling in the desert outside  of las vegas than at the lake and that  the danger of submerged objects is no  greater now than any other time as  always boaters just have to pay  attention it's true that few other  marinas including coalville bay have had  to temporarily suspend boat launches but  marina services remain open including  the slips that house private watercraft  there are fourteen hundred of those  slips at lake mead marina where kids  still feed swarming fish from the dock  and their parents rent boats to cruise a  few miles to check out hoover dam  boat owners usually don't have to wait  more than 20 minutes to get their craft  into the water far different from news  reports saying it takes four hours the  long term scenario for lake mead is  challenging because the overextended  colorado river is the most endangered  river in the country as it has been for  decades but for now and into the future  lake mead is still a premier watery  playground we're open for business we  want you to know that lake meat is still  here george knapp 8 news now
 

